  "checks": [
    "The language exposes native-owned custom, built-in and default EmojiPack objects, independent shares, SVG insert/replace with counted bytes, typed prefix find and explicit pack use by GlyphRun.",
    "Native tests exercise reference saturation, allocation failures during create, insert, cache load and default initialization, UTF-8 matching, concurrent default mutation and reallocation, SVG provider reentry and draw-thread disposal. Linux additionally ran instrumented aggregate/XRT TSan and aggregate UBSan.",
    "Graphics tests shape an explicit pack, close its original view and verify the glyph's red PNG pixels with render target readback. A separate C probe in that rendered frame verifies that only the publication reference remains before a worker unpublishes the pack. The graphics thread retains the cached texture until retirement. Windows and Linux pass JIT and standalone dynamic/static AOT. Linux uses Xvfb and software GL.",
    "The matched native and xlang warm loops each perform 1000 searches on a prebuilt input, produce the same 4000-byte sum and make zero XRT allocations, reallocations or frees, for custom and built-in packs. Constructing the literal in the loop separately measured one string allocation per iteration. The comparison does not measure total call time. Checked view, span and synchronization work remain.",
    "Custom payload storage adds a reference counter to an existing allocation and the cold renderer cache allocates a retained SVG cache node. The warm draw takes the existing pack mutex on acquire and release. Existing host framebuffer, EGL and shape regressions run against this same candidate."
  ]
